  "id": "DrugBank_DB05013 - MONDO_6563",
  "verbalization": "Ingenol Mebutate is associated with Inverted Follicular Keratosis through a series of interconnected pathways, primarily involving the treatment of Actinic Keratosis (AK). The drug's indication for AK is linked to its role in modulating cellular processes that contribute to the development of this condition. Specifically, Ingenol Mebutate is known to inhibit the proliferation of abnormal keratinocytes, which is a key mechanism in the treatment of AK. This inhibition is supported by the drug's ability to induce apoptosis in dysplastic cells, a process that is relevant to the pathogenesis of Inverted Follicular Keratosis.\n\nThe evidence also suggests that Ingenol Mebutate may influence the metabolic pathways associated with Aminolevulinic Acid and Masoprocol, which are involved in the production of heme and the regulation of cellular differentiation. These pathways are implicated in the development of keratosis and may contribute to the pathophysiology of Inverted Follicular Keratosis. The presence of multiple indirect pathways linking Ingenol Mebutate to Inverted Follicular Keratosis underscores the potential for the drug to exert a therapeutic effect through a complex network of cellular mechanisms.\n\nWhile the evidence is robust and consistent across different pathways, the exact molecular mechanisms by which Ingenol Mebutate influences these pathways remain to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways suggests that the drug may have a role in the treatment of Inverted Follicular Keratosis by targeting cellular proliferation and differentiation processes, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm this hypothesis.",
